  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 236 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nLow pressure propagates eastward into Wisconsin and begins to\nproduce showers and storms across southern Wisconsin on Monday \nafternoon, with some discrepancies in solutions for the center of \nthe low. Generally modeling is coming into line with the center of\nthe low across central to southern Wisconsin. In the warm sector \nof the low, there is some potential for increasing instability \n(MUCAPE up to 1500 J/kg) along with increasing shear (bulk shear \nup to 30 kt) and temperatures in the 80s, so an isolated stronger \nstorm cannot be ruled out as the low passes through the region \n(hail as the main threat). With the timing of the cold front \nexpected to be overnight Monday, severity looks to be very \nlimited. \n\nThe main cold frontal passage looks to progress through \nthe region very early Tuesday morning, bringing gusty \nnorthwesterly winds and an end to precipitation. Highs in the low\n70s are expected, with northwesterly winds gradually diminishing\nand skies clearing as the front exits. High pressure will\nbuild into the Upper Great Lakes Tuesday night, with stronger low\npressure developing in the southern Plains by Wednesday morning.\nWarm frontogenesis from this new low in the Plains may bring \nincreasing cloud cover and isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms across southern Wisconsin Wednesday afternoon through \nThursday.\n\nStrong low pressure in the Plains and building high pressure in the\nGreat Lakes will continue to dominate the weather pattern through\nthe remainder of the week as the low deepens and the pressure \ngradient increases over southern Wisconsin. Winds are expected to \nbecome generally easterly and increase Thursday as low pressure \nejects into the Great Lakes region. The strength of the low \ncontinues to be in question, with the GFS the most intense \nsolution with a deep, strong low over northern Wisconsin. However,\nthe Canadian and Euro show a stronger high and a slow low \nprogression. Knowing the GFS's bias toward overdeepening lows in \nthe presence of a strong high, lean toward the Canadian/Euro \nsolutions, which do keep southern Wisconsin dry through Thursday \nbefore the high finally propagates eastward. Best chances (50-60%)\nexpected Thursday afternoon into Thursday night, with general\nagreement that low pressure will exit to the east into Friday,\nbringing gusty northwesterly winds and a much colder airmass to\nthe region into the weekend. By Saturday, with dry, cold air\nmoving in from northern Canada, potential for frost or even freeze\nincreases.\n\nMH",
